## 9. Failure triage instead of blanket replanning (correction 10)

`s_triage.ts` is entered on every thrown error and every negative human verdict. It classifies, records an `Incident`, and routes:

```ts
interface Incident { at: string; state: State; class: FailureClass;
                     message: string; excerpt: string; action: TriageAction; }
```

| Class | Detection | Route |
|---|---|---|
| `TRANSIENT` | DNS/ECONNRESET/5xx/timeout on fetch, push, health poll | Retry same state, exponential backoff, max 4 |
| `CREDENTIALS` | Git auth prompt, HTTP 401/403, login selector reached but form rejected | Re-ask credentials (secure input), then resume same state |
| `CONFIG` | Build/run command not found, port mismatch, health 404 | Back to S3 to correct `jira-flow.yml`, then resume |
| `SELECTOR` | Playwright timeout locating a known element | Manual handoff (§10.3) or profile edit, resume mid-run |
| `IMPLEMENTATION_DEFECT` | Compile/test failure after fix attempts exhausted | Fix loop first; then S10 with failure context; replan only if it recurs |
| `DESIGN_DEFECT` | Regression failure tied to an AC, or user says the approach is wrong | Back to S8 `PLAN` with incident context, `replanCount++` |
| `INFRA_UNAVAILABLE` | Dev env down, runner not reachable | Pause: persist and wait for the user, no state loss |
| `USER_REJECT` | Plan or diff rejected | Back to S8 with feedback |

Every triage render includes `[Retry] [Fix config] [Replan] [Pause] [Abort]`, so "redo from planning" is always one click away — it is just no longer the automatic response to a flaky network. Replan prompts receive the last incidents, the previous plan, and the current diff so the planner revises rather than restarts blind.

