## 10. Human checkpoints, credentials, and the browser

### 10.1 Checkpoint mechanics (correction 3)

Buttons execute commands; a command has no access to the original `ChatResponseStream`. So every checkpoint is designed to be satisfied **either** by a typed reply **or** by a button that (a) mutates the persisted session, (b) shows a toast confirming what it recorded, and (c) invokes `workbench.action.chat.open` with a prefilled `@jira-flow /continue`. No stage ever assumes a command callback can emit an assistant turn.

Deterministic intent parsing runs before any LLM call, so `approve`, `done`, `pass`, `fail`, `redo`, `/abort`, URLs, and format choices are never model-interpreted. Prose for structured fields (ticket details, Git strategy) falls back to a schema-constrained extraction call, then a confirmation table.

### 10.2 Credentials (correction 7)

`s17_collectCredentials.ts` never accepts secrets from the chat prompt.

```
I need test-runner credentials. These are entered in VS Code's secure input,
stored in the OS keychain for this session, and deleted when the session closes.
[Enter kID & password]   [Enter clientId & secret]
```

Each button: `showInputBox({ prompt, password: true, ignoreFocusOut: true })` → `context.secrets.store('jiraFlow.<sessionId>.<name>', value)` → session records only the key name.

Rules enforced in code:

- Secrets are read from `secrets` immediately before use and held in a local for the duration of one `fill()`.
- `util/redact.ts` masks every live secret value (plus `Authorization:`, `password=`, `secret=`, `token=` patterns) in **all** sinks: output channel, log files, chat, report, exception messages.
- Secrets are never placed in an LLM message. The context builder masks values of secret-shaped config keys while keeping the keys visible.
- Screenshots are taken **after** an explicit `page.fill` of password fields only when the field is masked by the app; otherwise the screenshot step is skipped for that view.
- `s23_close` and abort both delete every key in `secretRefs` and verify deletion.
- MFA/SSO/CAPTCHA/hardware token: detected as a `SELECTOR`/`CREDENTIALS` class and routed to manual handoff — the agent cannot and should not automate a second factor.
- The prompt text recommends dedicated short-lived test credentials, not personal ones.

### 10.3 Regression (correction 8)

No universal profile. One profile per test-runner application, versioned in the repo at `.jira-flow/profiles/<name>.json` and referenced by `exec.regression.profile`:

```json
{
  "name": "payments-runner", "version": 1,
  "login":       { "path": "/login", "user": "#kid", "password": "input[type=password]",
                   "submit": "button[type=submit]", "success": "[data-testid=dashboard]", "timeoutSeconds": 60 },
  "clientAuth":  { "path": "/settings/client", "clientId": "#clientId", "clientSecret": "#clientSecret",
                   "submit": "button:has-text('Connect')", "success": "text=Connected" },
  "api":         { "nav": "a:has-text('API')", "path": "/api", "ready": "[data-testid=api-suite]" },
  "regression":  { "start": "button:has-text('Run Regression')", "running": "[data-testid=running]",
                   "complete": "[data-testid=run-complete]", "timeoutSeconds": 3600,
                   "summary": { "total": "[data-testid=total]", "passed": "[data-testid=passed]", "failed": "[data-testid=failed]" } },
  "download":    { "trigger": "button:has-text('Download results')", "extensions": [".xlsx", ".csv", ".json", ".zip"] }
}
```

- `jiraFlow.recordProfile` command launches `npx playwright codegen <url>` and writes a scaffold from what the user captures.
- **Profile validation gate**: a profile must pass a `jiraFlow.validateProfile` dry run (login + navigate + locate start button, no run) against a staging runner before it is allowed to drive an autonomous regression. Unvalidated profile → the agent runs in step-confirm mode instead.
- Run: headed Chromium, fresh non-persistent context, `acceptDownloads: true`, per-step `stream.progress`, screenshot per step.
- **Manual handoff on any miss**: on selector timeout the agent saves a screenshot + DOM excerpt, leaves the browser open, and asks `[I did this step — continue] [Update profile & retry] [Fail]`. Manual steps are recorded in `regression.manualSteps` and appear in the report, so the audit trail distinguishes automated from human actions.
- `s19_collectResults.ts`: `waitForEvent('download')` paired with the trigger click, `saveAs` under `exec.report.outputDir`, unzip if needed, tolerant parse of total/passed/failed/skipped (file first, on-page selectors second, unknown third), final full-page screenshot, browser closed unless failures exist.
- `s20_awaitTestVerdict.ts`: the parsed counts are shown, but **the user decides** pass/fail (flakes are real). `fail` → triage as `DESIGN_DEFECT` with the failing test names attached.

