## 14. Residual risks

| Risk | Mitigation in this plan |
|---|---|
| Force-sync destroys uncommitted work | S5 gate, stash default offer, typed phrase for discard, `headShaAtStart` logged, stash reported at close |
| Repo too large for one context | Full structural inventory + scored retrieval + planner-requested reads; coverage warnings when AC map is thin |
| Agent weakens the app to pass its own check | Health config is user-confirmed; agent refuses to add/expose actuator endpoints |
| Credentials leaking into chat history, logs, or prompts | Secure inputs only, keychain storage, global redactor, secrets excluded from LLM context, wipe on close |
| Unknown runner UI | Per-runner validated profile, step-confirm mode when unvalidated, manual handoff recorded in the report |
| Untested commit from the report | Artifact-first; docs-only commit is opt-in and labelled |
| Transient failures burning full replan cycles | Triage classification; replan reserved for design defects and user rejection |
| Window reload killing the session | Reload removed from the happy path; session on disk; Resume via notification + prefilled chat |

The core idea holds: a deterministic, persisted state machine driving a chat participant is the right architecture for this workflow. What v2 changes is that the dangerous parts — Git history, workspace identity, execution commands, secrets, browser assumptions, commit ordering, and failure response — are now explicit contracts with human gates rather than inferred behaviour.
